In this study, I have explored my gender identity through the lens of scientific rationality, materialism, and empirical evidence. By integrating autobiographical records, memories, and the predictive processing framework, I have traced how external cultural categories can become embodied components of the self. I also discussed the implications of this naturalistic constructivist view for transgender people, highlighting how it challenges traditional essentialist and post-structuralist perspectives.

For transgender individuals like myself who understand their experience through physical reality and personal experience, the process of social acquisition does not diminish the authenticity of those experiences. An experience can be acquired, internalised, and deserving of care, while the conditions that sustain its reproduction remain fully open to scientific and philosophical critique. It is precisely this distinction that allows us to respect the individual in the present while envisioning and advocating for a different future.